diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ef21a62
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+# Posting as a chat bot
+
+In **Account settings**, choose **Set up chat bots** and then **Add a chat bot**. Copy the **Bot code** from the bot list. You can manage bots you create; account administrators can manage all bots.
+
+Administrators can change **Bots per member** in **Account settings**. The default is five bots per member, and zero prevents members from creating bots. Deleted bots free up space, and lowering the limit leaves existing bots in place. Administrators aren't limited.
+
+Bots don't join open rooms by default. Give a bot access to a specific room in that room's settings, or enable **Add to all open rooms** on the bot to grant access to all current and future open rooms. Turning the switch off leaves existing memberships in place.
+
+Use the bot code and the room ID (from the room's URL) to post from a script or CI job:
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/${ROOM_ID}/${BOT_CODE}/messages"
+```
+
+The request body is UTF-8 text, not a form or JSON object. A successful post returns `201 Created` with a `Location` header pointing to `/messages/:id`. The API returns `401 Unauthorized` for an invalid or revoked code, `404 Not Found` for a room the bot cannot post in, and `422 Unprocessable Content` for an empty message.
+
+Treat the bot code like a password: use HTTPS, store it in your CI secret store, and don't print the request URL in shell traces. Campfire redacts the code from Rails request logs and disables raw request logging in its bundled proxy. If you run another proxy, make sure it also redacts these URLs or doesn't log them.
+
+Deleting a bot revokes its code without removing its previous messages. Codes are purpose-specific signed IDs, so changing Campfire's `SECRET_KEY_BASE` also invalidates previously copied bot codes.
+
+## Receiving @mentions
+
+Set **Webhook URL** when creating or editing a bot to receive callbacks for mentions and room joins. Leave it blank and save to turn callbacks off. Room members can select the bot from the @-mention suggestions in any room the bot belongs to.
+
+Campfire sends one `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json` per mentioned bot when someone posts a new message. Delivery runs in a background job; the person posting doesn't wait for the webhook.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": { "text": "restart the build please", "html": "<div>… full rich-text body …</div>" },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`message.body.text` omits the receiving bot's mention; `message.body.html` keeps the full rich text. Resolve `message.link` and `room.reply_to` relative to your Campfire origin. `room.reply_to` contains your bot's posting credential: keep it out of logs and don't share it.
+
+## Joining rooms
+
+When a bot with a webhook gains access to an open or closed room, Campfire sends a `joined` callback. Direct conversations never send this event. Campfire attempts delivery once per bot and room; saving memberships again or removing and re-adding a bot that was already notified doesn't send another event. Adding a webhook does not send events for existing memberships.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+## Replying to callbacks
+
+Both `mention` and `joined` callbacks support immediate or later replies:
+
+- To reply immediately, respond with `200 OK` and a UTF-8 body with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html`. Campfire posts the response as the bot, sanitizing HTML replies.
+- To reply later, return `204 No Content` and send a plain-text `POST` to `room.reply_to` when ready. You can also return `204` without replying.
+
+Use a public HTTP(S) endpoint, preferably HTTPS. Campfire does not connect to private addresses or follow redirects. Delivery times out after 10 seconds and is not retried; immediate replies must be at most 256 KiB.
+
+## Posting limits and suspension
+
+Each bot can post up to 10 messages in a rolling 10-second window across all its rooms. Posts to the API and immediate webhook replies share the same limit. The first excess attempt starts a 30-second cooldown. API posts return `429 Too Many Requests` with `Retry-After` (whole seconds remaining, rounded up), and immediate replies are dropped. The cooldown is not extended by further attempts. Five more attempts during that cooldown suspend the bot; subsequent API requests return `403 Forbidden`.
+
+A bot is also suspended after 10 consecutive failed webhook deliveries, counting both `mention` and `joined` callbacks. Non-2xx responses, timeouts, and connection errors are failures. Any 2xx response resets the streak, even if it doesn't produce a chat message.
+
+Suspended bots receive no new callbacks. They keep their room memberships and messages, count toward the owner's bot limit, and appear as **Suspended** with the reason on the **Chat bots** page. An administrator can select **Reactivate** and confirm to reset the bot's limits and resume posting and delivery with its existing code.
diff --git a/lib/bot_message_request.rb b/lib/bot_message_request.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d21df39
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/bot_message_request.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+class BotMessageRequest
+  PATH = %r{(/rooms/+[^/?]+/+)[^/?]+(/+messages)(?=/*(?:\?|\z))}
+
+  def self.filter_path(path)
+    path.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && PATH.match?(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      # Even curl's default form content type carries literal text on this route.
+      # Set this before Rack::MethodOverride or Rails can parse it as parameters.
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain"
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+end
